## Introduction

A kernel is written for one point of an iteration space, and a dispatch runs it for every point. SPEC-04P.2 6.13 defines the meaning of a dispatch by a sequential procedure: check the arguments, run the work-items one at a time in ascending order, complete the reductions and publish the outputs only if nothing faulted. An executor that runs work-items in another order, or many at once on a GPU, has to reach the outcome of that procedure by other means: the same output bytes, the same reduction results, the same fuel consumed and, when something faults, the same fault record, down to the work-item and the operation inside it.

This first example dispatches one kernel. `scale` multiplies each element of `a` by `k`; the program runs it twice:

```cint scale.ci
kernel scale[n](in I64[n] a, in I64 k, out I64[n] b) over [i: n] {
    b[i] = a[i] * k;
}

void run() {
    I64[4] a = [1, -2, 3, 4];
    I64[4] b;
    scale(a, 3, b);
    "{b[0]} {b[1]} {b[2]} {b[3]}\n";
    a[1] = 4611686018427387904;
    a[3] = -4611686018427387904;
    scale(a, 3, b);
    "{b[0]} {b[1]} {b[2]} {b[3]}\n";
}

run();
```

```text
3 -6 9 12
fault[E_OVERFLOW]: the exact result is outside the type's range
  --> scale.ci:2:17
   |
 2 |     b[i] = a[i] * k;
   |                 ^ mul.checked.i64
   |
   = operation: mul.checked.i64
   = operand-count: 2
   = operand: I64 4611686018427387904
   = operand: I64 3
   = exact:   13835058055282163712
   = limit:   I64 9223372036854775807
   ...
   = address: scale.scale, dispatch 1, phase 1, work-item 1, step 2
   ...
```

## 1 Scope

1. This document specifies, for programs in `cint-core-1`, the language profile that SPEC-04P.2 defines:
   - the phases of a dispatch, the numbering of dispatches and the checks a dispatch makes before any work-item runs, in their order;
   - the step ordinal, and which of several faults in one dispatch is reported;
   - the test that decides whether two arguments of a dispatch may overlap;
   - the staging and publication of outputs;
   - the evaluation of a work-item and the fuel it is charged;
   - the evaluation of reductions inside kernels and the attribution of their faults;
   - what every executor shall and shall not do, and what each executor of cint 0.2.0 runs and refuses.
2. This document does not specify:
   - the form and meaning of kernels, arrays and views as a program sees them, which SPEC-04P.2 6.12 and 6.13 specify;
   - the host interface through which a host program registers memory, places it on a device and issues dispatches, which SPEC-03P.1 specifies;
   - the byte encodings of fault records, which SPEC-01P.2 specifies;
   - the intermediate representation, the C that the compiler emits and the conformance suite, which SPEC-09P.1 specifies;
   - speed. No performance figure is given, and none is claimed.

### 5.2 Dispatch numbers

Every dispatch of an entry has a number, its position among the dispatches of that entry. The number is part of the address of a fault in the dispatch.

**Semantics**

1. <a id="F-1"></a>Each entry counts its dispatches. The first dispatch of an entry has number 0, the next 1 and so on, in program order. Every dispatch takes the next number, whether it succeeds or faults, and a dispatch that faults in its entry phase takes one too. A dispatch that the host interface refuses before it begins, because its arguments cannot be formed into values at all, takes none (SPEC-03P.1) [F-1] [SPEC-04P.2 P-3, IM-112].
2. The count restarts at 0 at every entry. It is not part of the program's state, and a checkpoint neither saves nor restores it [F-1] [SPEC-04P.2 IM-112].
3. A fault outside any dispatch has no address at all; no dispatch number stands for "not in a kernel" [F-1].
4. <a id="F-2"></a>A dispatch has one number even when an executor splits it into several launches [F-2].

**Examples**

5. One entry runs three dispatches of one kernel. The first two double both elements; the third overflows at work-item 1:

