1. <a id="H-14b"></a>A host records a context through a recorder, which makes the host's calls on the context and writes their events. Beginning a recording writes its header, the code of the program, and a checkpoint of the context as it is. Each entry, checkpoint, restore and cleared fault is then made through the recorder, and print statements are written as effects. Ending the recording writes its last event. A context is recorded by one recorder at a time. In cint 0.2.0 a recorded entry takes integer and `Bool` arguments only: an entry with an array argument is refused while recording [H-14b].
2. <a id="H-14"></a>A recording consists of a header, which holds the profile, the fuel model, the runtime contract, the revision identity and the recording mode; the code of each revision it runs; the initial checkpoint; and the stream of events [H-14]:
   - `entry`: its sequence number, the export's name, its canonical arguments, its fuel budget, depth limit and frame-arena capacity;
   - `effect`: the bytes of a print statement and the status the host returned;
   - `outcome`: the status, the fault record bytes if any, the fuel consumed, the result or the error result and the state hash;
   - `checkpoint`, `restore` and `clear_fault`, with the state hashes and the record they concern;
   - `end`, written last, with the head of the chain of digests over the events and the number of events. A recording without it is incomplete and is never loaded in part.
3. <a id="H-18"></a>A recording is in `full` mode unless `digest` is asked for. A `full` recording reproduces a run on its own; a `digest` recording holds digests in place of input contents and checks a run whose inputs are supplied again [H-18].
4. <a id="H-17"></a><a id="H-17a"></a>Replay checks the recording whole, its checkpoints and its chain of digests, before it reads any of it. It then restores the initial checkpo

