"done\n";
```

Lesson 101-08 shows a loop that can count down.

## the program

Here is a rule: if a number is even, halve it; if it is odd, triple it and add one. Start from 27 and repeat until the number reaches 1:

```ci
// Halve it if even, triple it and add one if odd, until it reaches 1.
I64 start = 27;
I64 n = start;
I64 steps = 0;
I64 highest = n;
while (n != 1) {
    if (n % 2 == 0) {
        n = n / 2;
    } else {
        n = 3 * n + 1;
    }
    steps += 1;
    if (n > highest) { highest = n; }
}
"{start} reaches 1 in {steps} steps, climbing as high as {highest}\n";
```

Run it:

```text
27 reaches 1 in 111 steps, climbing as high as 9232
```

The numbers rise and fall like a hailstone in a storm cloud before they come down to 1, which is why the list of numbers is called a hailstone sequence. Every positive whole number anyone has tried comes down to 1 in the end, but nobody has proved that every one does.

## what happened

**`while` repeats as long as its condition is true.** Before each pass, cint checks `n != 1`. While it is true, the lines in the braces run again. When `n` reaches 1, the condition is false and the program goes on to the line after the loop. Nobody knows in advance how many passes that takes: for 27 it is 111. That is the difference from lesson 101-07's `for`, which counts a set number of passes.

**Something inside the loop must change the condition.** Each pass changes `n`. A loop whose condition never becomes false runs until something else stops it.

**`steps` and `highest` keep a running tally.** `steps += 1;` counts the passes. `highest` starts at 27, and whenever `n` climbs above it, `highest` takes the new value.

**A `while` loop can count down.** A `for` range only counts up, so counting down is a job for `while`:

```ci
I64 n = 5;
while (n > 0) {
    "{n}\n";
    n -= 1;
}
"liftoff\n";
```

This prints 5, 4, 3, 2 and 1, one to a line, then `liftoff`.

## stopping early, skipping a pass

`break;` leaves a loop at once. `continue;` skips the rest of the current pass and goes on to the next one:

```ci
// The first power of 2 above 1000.
I64 x = 1;
while (true) {
    x *= 2;
    if (x > 1_000) {
        break;
    }
}
"first power of 2 above 1000: {x}\n";

// The numbers from 1 to 10 that 3 does not divide.
for n in 1..=10 {
    if (n % 3 == 0) {
        continue;
    }
    "{n} ";
}
"\n";
```

```text
first power of 2 above 1000: 1024
1 2 4 5 7 8 10 
```

`while (true)` would run forever, so the `break` inside it is the only way out. In the second loop, `continue` skips the print for 3, 6 and 9.

