A literal that cannot fit its type is a compile error, C2003. For example, `U8 b = 256;` is refused.

## the program

This program uses the 32-bit FNV-1a hash. Its multiply is meant to wrap, so the program writes `*%`:

```ci
// FNV-1a, a 32-bit hash. Hashes wrap on purpose, so the
// multiply is written *% and says so.
const U32 FNV_OFFSET = 2_166_136_261;
const U32 FNV_PRIME = 16_777_619;

U32 fnv1a[n](in U8[n] bytes) {
    U32 h = FNV_OFFSET;
    for i in 0..n {
        h = h ^ (bytes[i] as U32);
        h = h *% FNV_PRIME;
    }
    return h;
}

void main() {
    U32 a = fnv1a("hello");
    U32 b = fnv1a("hellp");
    "hello -> {a:x}\n";
    "hellp -> {b:x}\n";
}
```

## three families of operators

| Family | Spelling | On overflow |
|---|---|---|
| checked | `+` `-` `*` `/` `<<` | faults |
| wrapping | `+%` `-%` `*%` | keeps the low bits, never faults |
| saturating | `+|` `-|` `*|` | stops at `T.min` or `T.max`, never faults |

This program prints four results:

```ci
U8 a = 10;
U8 b = 20;
U8 w = a -% b;
U8 s = a -| b;
I8 m = 100;
I8 t = m *| 3;
I8 u = m *% 3;
"{w} {s} {t} {u}\n";
```

```text
246 0 127 44
```

10 - 20 wraps to 246 in `U8` and saturates to 0; 100 * 3 saturates to 127 in `I8` and wraps to 44 (300 - 256).

