### fix 3: report instead of stopping

Put the first version back once more. This fix keeps the `I32` total, but turns the overflow into an error that the program handles. `add_result` returns the sum, or the error `overflow` when the sum does not fit, and `try` passes that error up to the caller:

```ci
ArithError!I32 sum_of_squares[n](in I32[n] xs) {
    I32 total = 0;
    for i in 0..n {
        total = try add_result(total, xs[i] * xs[i]);
    }
    return total;
}
```

```ci
    I32 squares = sum_of_squares(day) catch (err) {
        "day {d:>2}: too large to summarize in I32\n";
        return;
    };
    I32 variance = squares / 1_440 - mean * mean;
```

```text
$ cint run weather.ci
...
day  8: low 7.58, high 15.09, mean 11.71, spread 1.93
day  9: too large to summarize in I32
day 10: too large to summarize in I32
...
day 30: too large to summarize in I32
```

The program finishes and exits with status 0, but it answers nothing after day 8, and days 5, 7 and 8 are still wrong. Turning a fault into an error is the right fix when some inputs are truly out of range. Here the readings are ordinary and only the total is too narrow, so the error hides the problem.

### run with one key

You can make Ctrl+Shift+B (Cmd+Shift+B on macOS) run whatever file is open. In your `cint-programs` folder, make a folder called `.vscode` and in it a file called `tasks.json`:

```json
{
  "version": "2.0.0",
  "tasks": [
    {
      "label": "cint run",
      "type": "shell",
      "command": "cint",
      "args": ["run", "${file}"],
      "group": { "kind": "build", "isDefault": true },
      "problemMatcher": {
        "source": "cint",
        "severity": "error",
        "fileLocation": ["relative", "${fileDirname}"],
        "pattern": [
          { "regexp": "^(error|fault)\\[(\\w+)\\]: (.*)$", "code": 2, "message": 3 },
          { "regexp": "^\\s+--> (.*):(\\d+):(\\d+)$", "file": 1, "line": 2, "column": 3 }
        ]
      }
